1. Problem Description
The selected community problem is the difficulty of coordinating waste collection in communities in Sierra Leone. Waste may accumulate around homes, markets, streets and drainage areas when collection requests are not recorded consistently, vehicles are unavailable, or the status of a collection is not tracked. This project proposes a logical Community Waste Management System that records collection requests, checks vehicle availability, assigns available vehicles and tracks whether a request is completed or remains pending.
The purpose of the project is to design how a system should work. It is not a requirement to write a working computer program. The solution is expressed through an algorithm, pseudocode, a flowchart, functional modules and sample outputs.
2. Background and Problem Analysis
2.1 The community problem
The central problem is that waste collection can be difficult to organize when there is no simple, consistent way to register requests and follow them through to completion. If information about a location or the collection status is missing, local coordinators may not know which areas need attention first. A request may also be delayed when no collection vehicle is available.
2.2 Who is affected?
Residents, market traders, community leaders, waste collectors and local authorities may all be affected. Residents and traders need cleaner surroundings; collection teams need clear information about where to go; and local authorities need a way to see which requests have been completed and which still require action.
2.3 Why the problem exists
Possible causes include incomplete reporting, poor coordination between communities and collection teams, limited vehicle availability, and a lack of consistent status updates. These are the conditions the proposed logical solution is designed to address; the report does not claim that one system alone can resolve every waste management challenge.
2.4 Why a logical solution is needed
A consistent process can ensure that each request is recorded, checked and given a clear status. When a vehicle is available, the request can be scheduled. When no vehicle is available, the request can remain on a pending list rather than being lost. After collection, the status can be updated so the coordinator can distinguish completed work from outstanding work.
3. Proposed Solution
The proposed Community Waste Management System is a simple request-management process for a community coordinator. The coordinator enters a location, waste type and estimated quantity. The system checks that the required details have been provided and registers the request. It then checks vehicle availability. If a vehicle is available, it is assigned and collection is scheduled. If no vehicle is available, the request is marked pending. For scheduled requests, the collection status is checked and updated. The system displays the result and can repeat the process for another request.
The initial design is intentionally limited to the core process. In a later version, the system could include priorities for urgent locations, a vehicle register, dates and times, notifications, and reports. Those additions would need their own requirements and logic.
4. Computational Thinking Process
4.1 Decomposition
The main waste collection problem is divided into smaller tasks: (1) collect the location and waste details; (2) validate the details; (3) register the request; (4) check vehicle availability; (5) assign a vehicle or mark the request pending; (6) record whether collection has happened; (7) update the status; and (8) display the result. Each task can be designed and checked separately, making the overall problem easier to manage.
4.2 Pattern recognition
Similar steps occur whenever a new collection request is made. Each request needs a location, waste details, a vehicle-availability check and a status. Repeated outcomes also appear: a vehicle is available or unavailable, and a scheduled collection is completed or still pending. Recognizing these repeated patterns allows the same decision process to be used for many requests.
4.3 Abstraction
The design focuses on information necessary to coordinate a request: request number, location, waste type, estimated quantity, vehicle availability and collection status. Personal details or unrelated information are not needed for the core process. This keeps the solution simple and focused on its purpose.
4.4 Algorithm design
The algorithm puts the tasks in a clear order and uses decisions to choose the correct path. It checks that request details are valid before registration, checks vehicle availability before assignment, and checks collection status before marking a request completed. Repetition allows the coordinator to process another request without restarting the whole process manually.
5. Algorithm Design
1.	START.
2.	Enter the community location, waste type and estimated quantity.
3.	Check whether all required details are valid.
4.	If any required detail is missing or invalid, display an error and ask for the details again.
5.	Register the valid waste collection request.
6.	Check whether a collection vehicle is available.
7.	If a vehicle is unavailable, mark the request as PENDING and display that no vehicle is available.
8.	If a vehicle is available, assign it to the request and schedule collection.
9.	Check whether the waste collection has been completed.
10.	If collection is completed, update the status to COMPLETED.
11.	If collection is not completed, keep the status as PENDING.
12.	Display the request status and result.
13.	Ask whether another request should be entered.
14.	If YES, repeat the process for the next request.
15.	If NO, END.
6. Pseudocode
CONSTANT COMPLETED = "COMPLETED"
CONSTANT PENDING = "PENDING"

FUNCTION RegisterRequest(location, wasteType, wasteQuantity)
    IF location is empty OR wasteType is empty OR wasteQuantity <= 0 THEN
        RETURN "INVALID"
    ENDIF
    SAVE request details
    RETURN "REGISTERED"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ProcessRequest()
    INPUT location
    INPUT wasteType
    INPUT wasteQuantity

    requestResult = RegisterRequest(location, wasteType, wasteQuantity)

    WHILE requestResult = "INVALID"
        DISPLAY "Invalid details. Enter the request again."
        INPUT location
        INPUT wasteType
        INPUT wasteQuantity
        requestResult = RegisterRequest(location, wasteType, wasteQuantity)
    ENDWHILE

    INPUT vehicleAvailable

    IF vehicleAvailable = "YES" THEN
        AssignVehicle(location)
        DISPLAY "Collection scheduled"
        INPUT collectionCompleted

        IF collectionCompleted = "YES" THEN
            UpdateCollectionStatus(COMPLETED)
            DisplayResult("Waste collection completed")
        ELSE
            UpdateCollectionStatus(PENDING)
            DisplayResult("Collection is still pending")
        ENDIF
    ELSE
        UpdateCollectionStatus(PENDING)
        DisplayResult("No vehicle available; request is pending")
    ENDIF
END FUNCTION

BEGIN
    INPUT anotherRequest
    WHILE anotherRequest = "YES"
        ProcessRequest()
        INPUT anotherRequest
    ENDWHILE
    DISPLAY "Waste request session ended"
END
