8. Logical Design Requirements
8.1 Input
The system receives the community location, waste type, estimated waste quantity, vehicle availability, collection completion status and whether another request should be processed.
8.2 Processing
The system validates required request details, registers a valid request, checks vehicle availability, assigns a vehicle when possible and updates the request status based on the collection outcome.
8.3 Output
Outputs include confirmation that a request has been registered, a collection schedule, a message that no vehicle is available, a pending status, or confirmation that collection is completed.
8.4 Variables, constants and data types
Name	Suggested data type	Purpose
location	Text/String	Community or collection location
wasteType	Text/String	Category of waste reported
wasteQuantity	Number	Estimated quantity; must be greater than zero
vehicleAvailable	Boolean or YES/NO	Whether a vehicle can be assigned
collectionCompleted	Boolean or YES/NO	Whether the scheduled collection occurred
collectionStatus	Text/constant	PENDING or COMPLETED
anotherRequest	Boolean or YES/NO	Controls repetition for additional requests
COMPLETED	Constant	Standard completed status
PENDING	Constant	Standard outstanding status
8.5 Decision structures
The design uses decisions to validate request details, determine whether a vehicle is available, and determine whether collection has been completed. Each decision has a clear outcome that directs the process to the next appropriate step.
8.6 Repetition structures
A WHILE loop repeats the request-entry process while required details are invalid. A second WHILE loop allows the coordinator to process multiple waste collection requests until the coordinator answers NO to the question about another request.
8.7 Boolean logic
The validation condition uses OR: if the location is empty OR the waste type is empty OR the quantity is not greater than zero, the request is invalid. Vehicle availability and collection completion use YES/NO conditions to select the next action.
8.8 Modular design
The solution separates registration, vehicle assignment, status updating and result display into modules. This makes each task easier to understand, test and improve without rewriting the whole process.
9. Functional Modules
RegisterRequest(location, wasteType, wasteQuantity): Checks required fields and records a valid request. Returns INVALID or REGISTERED.
CheckVehicleAvailability(): Receives or checks the availability status of a collection vehicle.
AssignVehicle(location): Links an available vehicle to the collection location and schedules the collection.
UpdateCollectionStatus(status): Stores PENDING or COMPLETED as the current request status.
DisplayResult(message): Displays a clear result to the coordinator.
ProcessRequest(): Coordinates the complete process for one request by calling the smaller functions.
