11. SDG Alignment
The project supports Sustainable Development Goal 11: Sustainable Cities and Communities, particularly the broad aim of making settlements more inclusive, safe, resilient and sustainable. Better coordination of waste collection can contribute to cleaner public spaces and improved management of waste. The project may also relate to SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) because cleaner surroundings can help reduce some environmental health risks. The strongest and primary alignment for this project is SDG 11.
12. Open-Source Justification
The proposed system could be developed using an open-source approach, meaning its source code and documentation would be made available under an appropriate open-source licence. This would allow qualified developers, students, civic groups and local authorities to inspect the design, report problems and contribute improvements. Potential benefits include lower licensing barriers, local adaptation, shared learning and less dependence on one developer or supplier.
Open source does not automatically mean that a project is secure, maintained or free of every cost. The project would still need responsible maintainers, documentation, testing, privacy safeguards and a plan for ongoing support.
13. Digital Public Goods Justification
The system could move toward becoming a Digital Public Good if it were designed to serve the public interest, made openly available under a suitable licence, documented clearly, and maintained in a way that supports reuse and responsible contribution. A practical version should be accessible on common devices, use understandable language, avoid unnecessary personal data and be adaptable to different communities.
A digital system is not automatically a Digital Public Good simply because it is free. Its licensing, openness, public benefit, safeguards and alignment with recognized DPG principles would need to be assessed.
14. Sierra Leone Community Impact and Sustainability
14.1 Expected community impact
The system is intended to make collection requests easier to organize, provide visibility into pending work and help coordinators use available vehicles more systematically. Residents and traders may benefit from more reliable coordination, while collection teams and local authorities may benefit from clearer records.
14.2 Beneficiaries
Potential beneficiaries include residents, market traders, community leaders, waste collection workers, local councils and community-based organizations involved in sanitation.
14.3 Long-term sustainability
For long-term use, the system would need clear responsibility for managing requests, updating collection statuses and maintaining records. It should be tested with local users, work under realistic connectivity conditions, protect any personal information it collects, and provide a way to report errors. A pilot in one community could identify practical problems before wider use.
14.4 Limitations
The logical design cannot create additional vehicles, guarantee that a scheduled collection will happen, or replace waste-management funding and public cooperation. It organizes information and decisions; real-world outcomes depend on staff, equipment, transport, maintenance and community participation.
15. Conclusion
This project has analyzed a community waste management problem and developed a logical solution rather than a computer program. Computational thinking was applied to break the problem into smaller tasks, identify repeated processes, focus on essential information and organize a step-by-step algorithm. The algorithm was expressed in pseudocode and a flowchart, supported by variables, constants, data types, decisions, repetition and functional modules. Sample scenarios show how the system can handle completed and pending requests. With appropriate development, community testing and maintenance, the proposed system could support more organized waste collection in Sierra Leone.
